Laura Kaminski relishing trophy hunt at Brann after Crystal Palace =E2=80=
=98crash course=E2=80=99 | Football | The Guardian


Laura Kaminski relishing trophy hunt at Brann after Crystal Palace =E2=80=
=98crash course=E2=80=99


Former Palace head coach has won a cup final in Norway and says chance to c=
hallenge for league title was a pull


=E2=80=98What head coach doesn=E2=80=99t want to compete for trophies?=E2=
=80=99: Laura Kaminski guided Brann to a cup final win against Aalesund Pho=
tograph: Marius Simensen/Bildbyr=C3=A5n/Shutterstock


Rich Laverty


=E2=80=9CWhen it=E2=80=99s a good day, it=E2=80=99s absolutely stunning, so=
 we=E2=80=99ll take that,=E2=80=9D Laura Kaminski says with a laugh about t=
he rainy west coast of Norway. The English former promotion-winning Crystal=
 Palace coach is referring to Bergen, the place she now calls home, whose f=
ootball club, Brann, she has taken to her heart since her appointment in Ja=
nuary.

She could easily be speaking about the job itself. After Brann failed to qu=
alify for the Women=E2=80=99s Champions League owing to a 4-0 second-leg de=
feat by Austria Vienna, they secured a cup final success against Aalesund, =
handing Kaminski a first major trophy on the continent. Her team are four p=
oints clear at the top of the league with seven games remaining.

At Brann, she is following in the footsteps of her fellow Briton, Martin Ho=
, and also Leif Smerud, who replaced her at Palace and who she succeeded at=
 Norway=E2=80=99s reigning champions. Kaminski was sacked by Palace in Febr=
uary 2025, midway through their first Women=E2=80=99s Super League campaign=
, and a move to Norway =E2=80=9Cwasn=E2=80=99t on the cards=E2=80=9D.

=E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99d never really thought about it because I=E2=80=99d alwa=
ys been involved in jobs in England, and then this opportunity came up whic=
h meant I was going to be stretched in terms of European football. Also com=
ing to an ambitious club that wants to fight for a title, that was a massiv=
e pull.=E2=80=9D


Laura Kaminski says the move to Brann happened suddenly: =E2=80=98One minut=
e you=E2=80=99re in London and 48 hours later you=E2=80=99re on a plane.=E2=
=80=99 Photograph: Marius Simensen/Bildbyr=C3=A5n/Shutterstock


After taking a much-needed break post-Palace, which she says gave her =E2=
=80=9Ca lot of time to reflect on what happened there=E2=80=9D, Kaminski wa=
s ready to go back into a No 1 job after becoming a head coach, rather than=
 an assistant, for the first time at Palace in 2024.

=E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99d had a great opportunity in WSL 1, I always wanted to c=
oach in top leagues, and here we can be in a title race most seasons, so I =
knew I=E2=80=99d have the chance to compete for trophies, and what head coa=
ch doesn=E2=80=99t want to compete for trophies?=E2=80=9D

Kaminski says it has been a =E2=80=9Cgreat learning curve=E2=80=9D having p=
reviously worked at promotion-winning Tottenham, for the England Women yout=
h teams and at Charlton before moving to Palace.

Domestic opportunities after Palace were forthcoming but Kaminski says: =E2=
=80=9CThe person has to match the project, and while things were coming up,=
 I didn=E2=80=99t feel they were a match. When Palace came up, that was a m=
atch for me, it was almost perfect in terms of personnel, being able to bui=
ld something; it was somewhere I could call home. And then my agent contact=
ed me and a recruitment agency got involved because Brann were looking for =
a new head coach, and it happened really quickly.

=E2=80=9CSometimes you don=E2=80=99t really realise what is going on until =
24 hours down the line, you=E2=80=99re moving to Norway. One minute you=E2=
=80=99re in London and 48 hours later you=E2=80=99re on a plane, but I snap=
ped it up because for me to get to a WSL club fighting for a title was goin=
g to be difficult, I=E2=80=99m still on a journey in management but I=E2=80=
=99ve been coaching for years.=E2=80=9D

More than 15, in fact. Kaminski gained her Uefa A licence in 2018. =E2=80=
=9CI always dreamed of being something that I couldn=E2=80=99t see,=E2=80=
=9D she says. =E2=80=9CThere were hardly any pro coaches when I started, le=
t alone female coaches. I don=E2=80=99t know why, I just believed that woul=
d happen.

=E2=80=9CI had that belief I was driven and ambitious enough to do it, but =
it was a labour of love because it was juggling multiple jobs, always on th=
e road, not making any money, sometimes paying to do the job. It=E2=80=99s =
come on a lot and thankfully now we=E2=80=99re in a different place where w=
e can leave that behind and reward people for their skills.=E2=80=9D

With growth has come more scrutiny, more pressure and a more ruthless sport=
, as Kaminski found out when Palace relieved her of her duties. Despite a s=
mall peak in results under Smerud, the club were relegated and Kaminski lea=
rned more in 18 months than in the previous 18 years.


Laura Kaminski on her time with Crystal Palace: =E2=80=98There are things I=
=E2=80=99ve reflected on where I think I could have controlled it better.=
=E2=80=99 Photograph: Tom Jenkins/The Guardian


=E2=80=9CIt was a course you couldn=E2=80=99t buy,=E2=80=9D she says. =E2=
=80=9CIt was led by Steve Parish, a great chair, great people at the club w=
ho were able to stretch me in the role. I met fantastic people; Palace will=
 always be my home. It=E2=80=99s a well-run club, but those 18 months were =
a crash course in how to function, especially when things aren=E2=80=99t go=
ing well, and things are stressful. There are things I=E2=80=99ve reflected=
 on, where I think I could have controlled it better, and there=E2=80=99s o=
ther things [that] I think were out of my hands.=E2=80=9D

Kaminski says =E2=80=9Cflying to games has been a huge change=E2=80=9D in N=
orway, where she is enjoying =E2=80=9Cfantastic scenery=E2=80=9D and senses=
 a country =E2=80=9Cvery proud of its football=E2=80=9D. Her obsession with=
 the sport remains.

=E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99m constantly watching games. I was addicted to football =
and still am. I=E2=80=99ll put on a Bundesliga game and see what I would do=
 at half-time in whatever scenario they=E2=80=99re in. I still have that hu=
nger and drive as when I did all those years out in the cold and the rain i=
n London. I was fortunate enough to work in some top clubs and with some to=
p people. By the time I got to Palace I found it easier than I first though=
t, or I should say smoother.

=E2=80=9CThe coaching part of being on the ground for so many years has giv=
en me so much knowledge, developing my way of playing over and over again, =
and who knows, that=E2=80=99s just the start. It will change and evolve; we=
=E2=80=99re playing some good football here. I=E2=80=99ve won in England, w=
on promotion with Tottenham and Palace, and now I want to prove I can do it=
 abroad.=E2=80=9D

